2
00:00:01,616 --> 00:00:03,660
Step 1, question by question.

3
00:00:03,892 --> 00:00:05,792
Today's set has 3 questions.

4
00:00:06,024 --> 00:00:08,148
Pause whenever you need to, and commit to

5
00:00:08,168 --> 00:00:10,108
an answer before the explanation.

6
00:00:11,267 --> 00:00:13,667
A 95% sensitive test.

7
00:00:13,901 --> 00:00:17,314
An 8.8% chance you actually have the disease.

8
00:00:17,546 --> 00:00:19,380
This one is biostatistics.

9
00:00:19,611 --> 00:00:20,840
Question 1 of 3.

10
00:00:22,033 --> 00:00:23,974
A new screening test for a disease has

11
00:00:23,994 --> 00:00:27,790
a sensitivity of 95% and a specificity of

12
00:00:27,810 --> 00:00:31,266
90%. The disease has a prevalence of 1%

13
00:00:31,286 --> 00:00:33,094
in the population being screened.

14
00:00:34,333 --> 00:00:36,102
Here is the finding that decides it.

15
00:00:36,336 --> 00:00:38,091
Prevalence: 1%

16
00:00:38,793 --> 00:00:41,588
If a person from this population tests positive,

17
00:00:41,833 --> 00:00:43,892
what is the probability that they actually have

18
00:00:43,912 --> 00:00:44,772
the disease?

19
00:00:45,459 --> 00:00:47,916
Pause here. Commit to an answer before the

20
00:00:47,936 --> 00:00:48,862
explanation.

21
00:00:53,426 --> 00:00:54,826
The answer is A.

22
00:00:55,137 --> 00:00:56,405
8.8%.

23
00:00:57,096 --> 00:00:59,984
In 10,000 people, 100 have the disease and

24
00:01:00,004 --> 00:01:03,892
95 of those test positive; of the 9,900

25
00:01:03,912 --> 00:01:07,431
without disease, 990 test positive falsely.

26
00:01:07,665 --> 00:01:11,987
Of 1,085 positives only 95 are true, giving

27
00:01:12,007 --> 00:01:16,617
95/1,085 = 8.8%.

28
00:01:17,863 --> 00:01:20,304
This is the sensitivity, which answers the

29
00:01:20,324 --> 00:01:22,672
opposite question: among people who have the

30
00:01:22,692 --> 00:01:25,158
disease, what fraction test positive.

31
00:01:25,392 --> 00:01:27,135
For B to be the answer, you would

32
00:01:27,155 --> 00:01:29,911
need this: A question asking for the proportion

33
00:01:29,931 --> 00:01:32,569
of diseased patients who test positive, not the

34
00:01:32,589 --> 00:01:34,937
proportion of positives who are diseased.

35
00:01:36,163 --> 00:01:38,775
This is the specificity: among people who are

36
00:01:38,795 --> 00:01:41,010
well, what fraction test negative.

37
00:01:41,245 --> 00:01:43,435
It ignores prevalence and runs in the wrong

38
00:01:43,455 --> 00:01:45,935
direction. For C to be the answer, you

39
00:01:45,955 --> 00:01:48,698
would need this: A question asking what fraction

40
00:01:48,718 --> 00:01:50,460
of healthy people test negative.

41
00:01:51,730 --> 00:01:55,065
A 50% positive predictive value would require

42
00:01:55,085 --> 00:01:57,236
prevalence high enough to balance the false

43
00:01:57,256 --> 00:02:00,331
positive rate. At 1% prevalence the false

44
00:02:00,351 --> 00:02:02,606
positives swamp the true positives.

45
00:02:02,838 --> 00:02:04,620
For D to be the answer, you would

46
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:07,120
need this: A prevalence roughly equal to the

47
00:02:07,140 --> 00:02:09,528
false positive rate, which at these test

48
00:02:09,548 --> 00:02:11,817
characteristics is about 10%.

49
00:02:13,096 --> 00:02:15,326
This is the false positive rate applied to

50
00:02:15,346 --> 00:02:18,128
the whole population, not a predictive value.

51
00:02:18,362 --> 00:02:20,486
It answers no question that was asked.

52
00:02:20,717 --> 00:02:22,434
For E to be the answer, you would

53
00:02:22,454 --> 00:02:25,290
need this: A question asking for the probability

54
00:02:25,310 --> 00:02:27,868
that a healthy person tests positive, which is

55
00:02:27,888 --> 00:02:31,025
10% here, not 0.9%.

56
00:02:32,196 --> 00:02:35,149
Sensitivity and specificity belong to the test.

57
00:02:35,383 --> 00:02:37,837
Predictive values belong to the patient in front

58
00:02:37,857 --> 00:02:40,007
of you, and they move with prevalence.

59
00:02:40,238 --> 00:02:42,850
Never answer a predictive-value question before

60
00:02:42,870 --> 00:02:44,388
writing down the prevalence.

61
00:02:45,630 --> 00:02:47,056
He fainted on the stairs.

62
00:02:47,290 --> 00:02:49,624
That single detail decides the question.

63
00:02:49,856 --> 00:02:51,480
This one is cardiology.

64
00:02:51,711 --> 00:02:53,006
Question 2 of 3.

65
00:02:53,712 --> 00:02:56,613
A 74-year-old man comes to the clinic because

66
00:02:56,633 --> 00:02:58,955
of a 6-month history of shortness of breath

67
00:02:58,975 --> 00:03:01,892
with exertion. Last week he lost consciousness

68
00:03:01,912 --> 00:03:04,155
while climbing the stairs to his apartment and

69
00:03:04,175 --> 00:03:05,667
recovered within a minute.

70
00:03:05,899 --> 00:03:07,246
He has no chest pain.

71
00:03:07,477 --> 00:03:12,917
Blood pressure 128/76 mmHg, pulse 72/min.

72
00:03:14,145 --> 00:03:16,770
Cardiac examination shows a harsh,

73
00:03:16,790 --> 00:03:19,546
crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur loudest at

74
00:03:19,566 --> 00:03:22,572
the right second intercostal space, radiating to

75
00:03:22,592 --> 00:03:25,628
both carotid arteries. The second heart sound is

76
00:03:25,648 --> 00:03:28,851
soft. The carotid upstroke is delayed and low

77
00:03:28,871 --> 00:03:31,378
in amplitude. The lungs are clear and there

78
00:03:31,398 --> 00:03:32,982
is no peripheral edema.

79
00:03:34,178 --> 00:03:35,947
Here is the finding that decides it.

80
00:03:36,181 --> 00:03:39,134
Carotid upstroke: delayed and low amplitude

81
00:03:39,845 --> 00:03:41,746
Which of the following best explains this

82
00:03:41,766 --> 00:03:43,627
patient's loss of consciousness?

83
00:03:44,328 --> 00:03:46,785
Pause here. Commit to an answer before the

84
00:03:46,805 --> 00:03:47,731
explanation.

85
00:03:52,295 --> 00:03:53,748
The answer is A.

86
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:56,710
Fixed stroke volume limiting the rise in cardiac

87
00:03:56,730 --> 00:03:58,130
output during exertion.

88
00:03:58,844 --> 00:04:01,535
A stenotic valve cannot open wider when demand

89
00:04:01,555 --> 00:04:04,169
rises. At rest the stiff ventricle still

90
00:04:04,189 --> 00:04:06,906
generates adequate output; on exertion stroke

91
00:04:06,926 --> 00:04:10,668
volume is capped, cardiac output fails to rise,

92
00:04:10,729 --> 00:04:12,523
and cerebral perfusion falls.

93
00:04:13,744 --> 00:04:16,330
Calcific aortic stenosis can extend into the

94
00:04:16,350 --> 00:04:18,738
conduction system, but a block is not

95
00:04:18,758 --> 00:04:21,460
load-dependent and does not wait for the stairs.

96
00:04:21,694 --> 00:04:23,359
For B to be the answer, you would

97
00:04:23,379 --> 00:04:26,385
need this: Syncope occurring at rest, or an

98
00:04:26,405 --> 00:04:29,635
ECG showing wide-complex bradycardia with absent

99
00:04:29,655 --> 00:04:30,515
P waves.

100
00:04:31,778 --> 00:04:34,612
Arrhythmic syncope classically has no warning

101
00:04:34,672 --> 00:04:36,967
and no reproducible exertional trigger.

102
00:04:37,201 --> 00:04:39,813
This patient's syncope is exertion-linked, which

103
00:04:39,833 --> 00:04:41,983
points to a haemodynamic cause.

104
00:04:42,215 --> 00:04:43,905
For C to be the answer, you would

105
00:04:43,925 --> 00:04:47,023
need this: Syncope without prodrome, family

106
00:04:47,043 --> 00:04:49,918
history of sudden cardiac death, scar on

107
00:04:49,938 --> 00:04:52,970
echocardiography, or a prolonged QT interval.

108
00:04:54,211 --> 00:04:57,085
Vasovagal syncope has a prodrome of warmth,

109
00:04:57,224 --> 00:05:00,124
nausea and tunnel vision, is provoked by heat,

110
00:05:00,448 --> 00:05:03,375
pain or prolonged standing, and does not produce

111
00:05:03,395 --> 00:05:05,756
a fixed low-amplitude carotid upstroke.

112
00:05:05,990 --> 00:05:07,694
For D to be the answer, you would

113
00:05:07,714 --> 00:05:10,602
need this: A clear prodrome, syncope while

114
00:05:10,622 --> 00:05:13,509
standing, rapid full recovery, and a normal

115
00:05:13,529 --> 00:05:15,048
cardiac examination.

116
00:05:16,311 --> 00:05:19,158
There is no volume loss, no diuretic use,

117
00:05:19,219 --> 00:05:20,658
and no positional trigger.

118
00:05:20,893 --> 00:05:23,544
Orthostatic syncope occurs within seconds of

119
00:05:23,564 --> 00:05:26,003
standing, not after climbing stairs.

120
00:05:26,235 --> 00:05:27,952
For E to be the answer, you would

121
00:05:27,972 --> 00:05:30,965
need this: A documented postural blood pressure

122
00:05:30,985 --> 00:05:34,728
drop, diuretic use or dehydration, and syncope

123
00:05:34,748 --> 00:05:35,503
on standing.

124
00:05:36,678 --> 00:05:39,750
Exertional syncope is a haemodynamic problem

125
00:05:39,770 --> 00:05:42,513
until proven otherwise: the heart cannot meet

126
00:05:42,533 --> 00:05:45,463
demand. Aortic stenosis, hypertrophic

127
00:05:45,483 --> 00:05:48,463
cardiomyopathy and pulmonary hypertension are

128
00:05:48,483 --> 00:05:49,633
the three causes.

129
00:05:50,844 --> 00:05:54,668
Platelets 18,000. Every instinct says transfuse.

130
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:56,208
That instinct is wrong.

131
00:05:56,439 --> 00:05:58,036
This one is hematology.

132
00:05:58,268 --> 00:05:59,629
Question 3 of 3.

133
00:06:00,319 --> 00:06:03,168
A 29-year-old woman comes in with five days

134
00:06:03,188 --> 00:06:05,799
of fatigue and easy bruising, and her husband

135
00:06:05,819 --> 00:06:08,285
says she has been confused since this morning.

136
00:06:08,519 --> 00:06:12,170
Temperature 37.8 C. There are petechiae over

137
00:06:12,190 --> 00:06:15,039
both shins. She is not pregnant and takes

138
00:06:15,059 --> 00:06:16,209
no medications.

139
00:06:17,486 --> 00:06:22,110
Hemoglobin 7.4 g/dL, platelets 18,000/uL,

140
00:06:24,328 --> 00:06:28,570
reticulocytes 9%, lactate dehydrogenase 900 U/L,

141
00:06:29,578 --> 00:06:32,808
indirect bilirubin 2.4 mg/dL, creatinine 1.1

142
00:06:32,828 --> 00:06:33,128
mg/dL. PT/INR and aPTT are both normal.

143
00:06:32,828 --> 00:06:33,128
Peripheral smear shows schistocytes.

144
00:06:32,828 --> 00:06:33,378
Urinalysis shows 1+ protein and no red cells.

145
00:06:48,053 --> 00:06:50,217
Which of the following is the most appropriate

146
00:06:50,237 --> 00:06:51,413
immediate treatment?

147
00:06:52,118 --> 00:06:54,574
Pause here. Commit to an answer before the

148
00:06:54,594 --> 00:06:55,520
explanation.

149
00:07:00,085 --> 00:07:01,419
The answer is A.

150
00:07:01,716 --> 00:07:03,261
Urgent plasma exchange.

151
00:07:03,964 --> 00:07:06,957
Plasma exchange removes the autoantibody against

152
00:07:06,977 --> 00:07:10,049
ADAMTS13 and replaces the enzyme at the same

153
00:07:10,069 --> 00:07:13,749
time. Untreated TTP carries high mortality and

154
00:07:13,769 --> 00:07:16,182
the response window is measured in hours.

155
00:07:17,464 --> 00:07:20,023
Every transfused platelet is fuel for further

156
00:07:20,043 --> 00:07:22,762
microthrombi. Platelet transfusion is

157
00:07:22,782 --> 00:07:25,381
contraindicated in TTP unless there is

158
00:07:25,401 --> 00:07:27,932
life-threatening bleeding or imminent surgery.

159
00:07:28,164 --> 00:07:29,960
For B to be the answer, you would

160
00:07:29,980 --> 00:07:32,630
need this: Active life-threatening haemorrhage,

161
00:07:32,756 --> 00:07:35,486
intracranial bleeding, or a patient going to the

162
00:07:35,506 --> 00:07:37,116
operating room immediately.

163
00:07:38,297 --> 00:07:41,606
Eculizumab blocks complement C5 and treats

164
00:07:41,626 --> 00:07:44,448
atypical haemolytic uraemic syndrome, which is

165
00:07:44,468 --> 00:07:47,198
renal-predominant with a rising creatinine and

166
00:07:47,218 --> 00:07:50,319
normal ADAMTS13 activity. This patient's

167
00:07:50,339 --> 00:07:53,056
creatinine is 1.1. For C to be the

168
00:07:53,076 --> 00:07:55,885
answer, you would need this: Renal-predominant

169
00:07:55,905 --> 00:07:59,267
disease with rapidly rising creatinine, dialysis

170
00:07:59,287 --> 00:08:02,515
requirement, and normal ADAMTS13 activity.

171
00:08:03,797 --> 00:08:06,948
Vitamin K treats coagulopathy, which prolongs

172
00:08:06,968 --> 00:08:10,872
the PT. This patient's PT and aPTT are

173
00:08:10,892 --> 00:08:13,990
both normal, so there is no coagulation factor

174
00:08:14,010 --> 00:08:16,385
deficiency to correct. For D to be the

175
00:08:16,405 --> 00:08:19,569
answer, you would need this: A prolonged PT

176
00:08:19,589 --> 00:08:22,806
or INR, as in warfarin use, liver disease

177
00:08:22,826 --> 00:08:24,252
or fat malabsorption.

178
00:08:25,497 --> 00:08:27,910
TTP is a medical emergency.

179
00:08:28,144 --> 00:08:30,559
Observation without plasma exchange was

180
00:08:30,579 --> 00:08:34,269
associated with mortality above 90% historically

181
00:08:34,289 --> 00:08:36,163
and remains unacceptable today.

182
00:08:36,394 --> 00:08:38,059
For E to be the answer, you would

183
00:08:38,079 --> 00:08:40,585
need this: A stable patient with no red

184
00:08:40,605 --> 00:08:43,202
cell destruction, which is not this picture.

185
00:08:44,397 --> 00:08:46,640
Microangiopathic haemolysis plus

186
00:08:46,660 --> 00:08:49,890
thrombocytopenia plus normal coagulation studies

187
00:08:49,910 --> 00:08:52,494
equals a thrombotic microangiopathy.

188
00:08:52,729 --> 00:08:54,945
Platelets are contraindicated and plasma

189
00:08:54,965 --> 00:08:58,208
exchange is the treatment: today, not tomorrow.

190
